  "provider_query": "English Wikipedia HTTP article: the default TCP port",
  "answer": "**Default TCP port for HTTP: 80** (unencrypted). Encrypted HTTPS uses **443**. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP rather than TCP.\n\nRelevant English Wikipedia sources:\n\n1. **HTTP** — TCP/IP connections use well-known ports, typically **port 80** if unencrypted and **port 443** if encrypted. Prior to HTTP/3, the usual transport is TCP. Example request–response traffic is shown for `www.example.com` on port 80.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP\n\n2. **Port (computer networking)** — By default, HTTP uses port **80** and HTTPS uses port **443**. Ports are often omitted from URLs unless non-default (e.g. `:8080`). Common-port table lists **80, 8080** for HTTP.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_(computer_networking)\n\n3. **List of TCP and UDP port numbers** — Port **80**: Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP); uses TCP in versions 1.x and 2. HTTP/3 uses QUIC on top of UDP. Port **443** is HTTPS (TCP for 1.x/2).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_TCP_and_UDP_port_numbers\n\n4. **Port 80** (disambiguation) — TCP port 80 is most often used by the Hypertext Transfer Protocol.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_80\n\n5. **Registered port** — Ports 0–1023 are system/well-known ports (the range that includes 80).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Registered_port\n\n6. **HTTP persistent connection** — Keep-alive reuses one TCP connection for multiple HTTP requests/responses (HTTP/1.1 default; HTTP/2 multiplexes over one connection).  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP_persistent_connection\n\n7. **Transport layer** — TCP is the main connection-oriented Internet transport and is used for protocols including HTTP web browsing.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport_layer\n\n8. **Open port** — An open port is a TCP or UDP port configured to accept packets so a service (such as a web server) can be reached.  \n   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_port",
